SportMeter makes it easier for athletes to share how they are doing. With short questions about enjoyment, energy, tension and involvement, athletes get a simple way to send signals. This means what is going on within the group no longer depends on one-off conversations, gut feeling or chance.

### Why we are taking action here

Many athletes do not naturally say how they are feeling. Sometimes because they find it difficult to say something, sometimes because the moment is not there, and sometimes because coaches simply cannot see everything. As a result, small signals stay under the radar for too long.

With SportMeter, we make that easier. Athletes answer short questions at a fixed moment. Not as a heavy questionnaire, but as a quick check on how things are going. This creates more visibility into enjoyment, motivation and areas for attention within the group, without athletes first having to start a conversation themselves.

### How does it work?

For athletes, SportMeter works from three simple places: your check-ins, your calendar and your coaching. This way you know when something is expected of you, you can briefly share how you are doing and you receive feedback around the themes your team is working on.

What does this mean for me?

1. You see your check-ins in one place. Think short questions about training sessions, matches, energy, recovery, enjoyment or how you feel within the group.

2. You complete a check-in when it is ready. This usually takes about 60 seconds. You choose a score, give a short answer or briefly explain something if you want to.

3. In your calendar, you see training sessions, matches and moments when a check-in or form is ready.

4. In coaching, you see your team’s focus point. You can make notes, keep something private or give feedback to your coach.

5. If you want to discuss something, you can send a signal to your coach, supervisor or confidential contact person.

### Together for Enjoyment

The strength of SportMeter lies in simplicity and repetition: athletes briefly share how they are doing, coaches more quickly gain visibility into what is going on and the club can follow up on signals more effectively. This way, wellbeing no longer depends on one-off conversations or gut feeling, but becomes a fixed part of coaching.

